window.__registerChapter({"num": 37, "slug": "037_Monogenic_Etiologies_of_Hypertension", "title": "Monogenic Etiologies of Hypertension", "authors": "Yosuke Miyashita, Joseph T. Flynn", "part_num": "VI", "part_title": "Renal Disorders", "word_count": 5781, "pearls": ["In contrast, secondary hypertension has an identifiable etiology, with kidney disease and renovascular disease being the most common causes in the pediatric age range.", "Liddle syndrome is a form of low-renin, low-aldosterone monogenic hypertension caused by pathogenic variants in the epithelial sodium channel (ENaC).", "Gordon syndrome is caused by pathogenic variants in one of 4 different genes, WNK1 , WNK4 , CUL3 , and KLHL3 .", "Inheritance is autosomal dominant, though there is an additional autosomal recessive form involving KLHL3 .", "After CUL3 , the order of phenotype severity based on gene variant is autosomal recessive KLHL3 , autosomal dominant KLHL3 , WNK4 , and WNK1 .", "AME is caused by deficiency of 11β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2 (11βHSD2).", "Congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH) represents a group of autosomal recessive disorders caused by cortisol biosynthesis defects, several of which are associated with hypertension (Table 37.3).", "The most common defect, 21-hydroxylase deficiency, which accounts for up to 90% of all CAH cases, does not cause hypertension."], "genes": ["CACNA1H", "CLCN2", "CUL3", "CYB11B1", "CYP11A1", "CYP11B1", "CYP11B2", "CYP17A1", "CYP21A2", "HCO3", "HSD11B2", "HSD3B2", "JAG1", "KCNJ5", "KLHL3", "KMT2D", "MYH6", "NCF1"], "omims": [], "subtopics": ["Monogenic Etiologies of Hypertension YOSUKE MIYASHITA, JOSEPH T. FLYNN", "Monogenic Hypertension With Low Plasma Aldosterone Levels", "Liddle Syndrome", "Gordon Syndrome", "Physical Findings Suggesting a Monogenic Disorder in a Child With Hypertension", "Physical Findings Suggesting a Monogenic Disorder in a Child With Hypertension—cont’d", "Syndrome of Apparent Mineralocorticoid Excess", "Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia", "Geller Syndrome", "Monogenic Hypertension With Elevated Plasma Aldosterone", "Glucocorticoid-Remediable Aldosteronism", "Familial Hyperaldosteronism Type 2", "Familial Hyperaldosteronism Type 3", "Familial Hyperaldosteronism Type 4"], "figs": [{"img": "fig_p0833_02.jpg", "label": "Fig. 37.1", "caption": "An overview of monogenic hypertension disorders categorized by salient biochemical and genetic features. APA, Aldosterone-producing adenoma; CAH, congenital adrenal hyperplasia; FH, familial hyperaldosteronism; GRA, glucocorticoid remediable aldosteronism; MEN, multiple endocrine neoplasia; PASNA, primary aldosteronism with seizures and neurological abnormalities; PHA, pseudohypoaldosteronism."}, {"img": "vfig_p0832_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 37.1", "caption": "An overview of monogenic hypertension disorders categorized by salient biochemical and genetic features. APA, Aldosterone-producing adenoma; CAH, congenital adrenal hyperplasia; FH, familial hyperaldosteronism; GRA, glucocorticoid remediable aldosteronism; MEN, multiple endocrine neoplasia; PASNA, primary aldosteronism with seizures and neurological abnormalities; PHA, pseudohypoaldosteronism."}, {"img": "fig_p0834_06.jpg", "label": "Fig. 37.2", "caption": "Molecular basis of Liddle syndrome. The epithelial sodium channel (ENaC) regulates sodium reabsorption in the distal tubules of the kidney. (A) ENaC, the renal outer medullary potassium channel (ROMK) and the sodium-potassium ATPase work in concert to maintain hydroelectrolytic balance in renal distal tubule epithelial cells. (B) pathogenic variants in Liddle syndrome result in either increased ENaC density on the apical membrane of the renal distal tubule epithelial cells or an increased propensity for channel opening, resulting in increased inward sodium current and altered flow of potassium, chloride, hydrogen ion, and bicarbonate (red arrows) . Pathophysiology of Liddle syndrome. AE1, Anion exchanger 1; ATPase, pump; Ca2þ, calcium; Cl , chloride; ENaC, epithelial sodium channel; Hþ, hydrogen ion; HCO3 , bicarbonate; Kþ, potassium; MR, mineralocorticoid receptor; Naþ, sodium; ROMK, renal outer medullary potassium."}, {"img": "vfig_p0833_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 37.2", "caption": "Molecular basis of Liddle syndrome. The epithelial sodium channel (ENaC) regulates sodium reabsorption in the distal tubules of the kidney. (A) ENaC, the renal outer medullary potassium channel (ROMK) and the sodium-potassium ATPase work in concert to maintain hydroelectrolytic balance in renal distal tubule epithelial cells. (B) pathogenic variants in Liddle syndrome result in either increased ENaC density on the apical membrane of the renal distal tubule epithelial cells or an increased propensity for channel opening, resulting in increased inward sodium current and altered flow of potassium, chloride, hydrogen ion, and bicarbonate (red arrows) . Pathophysiology of Liddle syndrome. AE1, Anion exchanger 1; ATPase, pump; Ca2þ, calcium; Cl , chloride; ENaC, epithelial sodium channel; Hþ, hydrogen ion; HCO3 , bicarbonate; Kþ, potassium; MR, mineralocorticoid receptor; Naþ, sodium; ROMK, renal outer medullary potassium."}, {"img": "fig_p0836_07.jpg", "label": "Fig. 37.3", "caption": "Adrenal steroidogenesis. DOC, Deoxycorticosterone; DHEA, dehydroepiandrosterone, StAR, steroidogenic acute regulatory protein. Enzymes whose deficiency is associated with hypertension are illustrated in red . *17-hydroxylase, 17,20 lyase, and 21-hydroxylase depend on P450 oxidoreductase (POR) for their catalytic activities."}, {"img": "vfig_p0835_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 37.3", "caption": "Adrenal steroidogenesis. DOC, Deoxycorticosterone; DHEA, dehydroepiandrosterone, StAR, steroidogenic acute regulatory protein. Enzymes whose deficiency is associated with hypertension are illustrated in red . *17-hydroxylase, 17,20 lyase, and 21-hydroxylase depend on P450 oxidoreductase (POR) for their catalytic activities."}, {"img": "fig_p0837_02.jpg", "label": "Fig. 37.4", "caption": "Adrenal steroidogenesis in 11β-hydroxylase deficiency. Font size shows relative increases and decreases in production."}, {"img": "vfig_p0836_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 37.4", "caption": "Adrenal steroidogenesis in 11β-hydroxylase deficiency. Font size shows relative increases and decreases in production."}, {"img": "fig_p0838_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 37.5", "caption": "Adrenal steroidogenesis in 17-α-hydroxylase deficiency. Font size shows relative increases and decreases in production."}, {"img": "vfig_p0837_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 37.5", "caption": "Adrenal steroidogenesis in 17-α-hydroxylase deficiency. Font size shows relative increases and decreases in production."}, {"img": "fig_p0839_02.jpg", "label": "Fig. 37.6", "caption": "Chimeric gene formation in glucocorticoid-remediable aldosteronism results from an unequal crossover event. The genes that encode aldosterone synthase (CYP11B2) and 11 β-hydroxylase: (CYP11B1) are both located on chromosome 8 within proximity of each other. The 5′ ACTH-responsive promoter region of the 11β-hydroxylase gene is fused to the 3′ coding sequence of the aldosterone synthase gene. As a result, aldosterone synthesis is abnormally regulated by ACTH rather than by the renin-angiotensin system. (From Halperin F, Dluhy RG. Glucocorticoid-remediable aldosteronism. Endocrinol Metab Clin North Am. 2011;40(2):333-341 [Fig. 2, p. 337].)"}, {"img": "vfig_p0838_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 37.6", "caption": "Chimeric gene formation in glucocorticoid-remediable aldosteronism results from an unequal crossover event. The genes that encode aldosterone synthase (CYP11B2) and 11 β-hydroxylase: (CYP11B1) are both located on chromosome 8 within proximity of each other. The 5′ ACTH-responsive promoter region of the 11β-hydroxylase gene is fused to the 3′ coding sequence of the aldosterone synthase gene. As a result, aldosterone synthesis is abnormally regulated by ACTH rather than by the renin-angiotensin system. (From Halperin F, Dluhy RG. Glucocorticoid-remediable aldosteronism. Endocrinol Metab Clin North Am. 2011;40(2):333-341 [Fig. 2, p. 337].)"}, {"img": "fig_p0840_04.jpg", "label": "Fig. 37.7", "caption": "Clinical and imaging findings in autosomal dominant hypertension with brachydactyly syndrome. (A) Severe brachydactyly is observed. (B) Radiograph of the hands shows a short length of the metacarpal bones in both hands and cone-shaped epiphyses. (C) Brain magnetic resonance angiogram (MRA) of the patient performed for headaches at 35 years of age, with right posterior cerebral artery (PCA) stenosis and left intracranial vertebral artery hypoplasia. (D–F) Brain diffusion-weighted imaging (DWI) performed at the first stroke event (at 42 years of age) shows a right pontine infarct, and MRA reveals stenosis of the basilar artery (BA) and bilateral PCA. (G and H) Repeat DWI obtained 1 day after stroke onset show additional infarct in the right thalamus and a gradient-recalled echo image reveals blooming artifact in the BA. (Continued)"}, {"img": "fig_p0842_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 37.8", "caption": "A diagnostic algorithm for early-onset hypertension. Determining plasma renin levels dictates consideration of monogenic hypertension: elevated renin levels suggest renovascular and other nonmonogenic forms of hypertension. Normal renin levels suggest familial pheochromocytoma/ paraganglioma or autosomal dominant hypertension with brachydactyly syndrome. Low renin levels are seen in the other forms of monogenic hypertension. When not specifically stated, any medications listed in the algorithm indicate a trial to assess responsiveness to the medication. AME, Apparent mineralocorticoid excess; CAH, congenital adrenal hyperplasia; DOC, deoxycorticosterone; FH, familial hyperaldosteronism; FHHt, familial hyperkalemia and hypertension syndrome (Gordon syndrome); GRA, glucocorticoid-remediable aldosteronism; HTNB, autosomal dominant hypertension with brachydactyly syndrome; MRA, mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist; PASNA, primary aldosteronism, seizures, and neurological abnormalities; PPGL, pheochromocytoma/paraganglioma."}, {"img": "vfig_p0841_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 37.8", "caption": "A diagnostic algorithm for early-onset hypertension. Determining plasma renin levels dictates consideration of monogenic hypertension: elevated renin levels suggest renovascular and other nonmonogenic forms of hypertension. Normal renin levels suggest familial pheochromocytoma/ paraganglioma or autosomal dominant hypertension with brachydactyly syndrome. Low renin levels are seen in the other forms of monogenic hypertension. When not specifically stated, any medications listed in the algorithm indicate a trial to assess responsiveness to the medication. AME, Apparent mineralocorticoid excess; CAH, congenital adrenal hyperplasia; DOC, deoxycorticosterone; FH, familial hyperaldosteronism; FHHt, familial hyperkalemia and hypertension syndrome (Gordon syndrome); GRA, glucocorticoid-remediable aldosteronism; HTNB, autosomal dominant hypertension with brachydactyly syndrome; MRA, mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist; PASNA, primary aldosteronism, seizures, and neurological abnormalities; PPGL, pheochromocytoma/paraganglioma."}, {"img": "fig_p0843_02.jpg", "label": "Fig. 37.9", "caption": "Venn diagram showing overlap of genes associated with left-sided obstructive lesions based on disease subtype. Asterisk indicates genes without robust evidence of association. AS, Aortic stenosis; BAV, bicuspid aortic valve; CoA, coarctation of the aorta; HLHS, hyperplastic left heart syndrome; IAA, interrupted aortic arch; LSOL, left-sided obstructive lesion. (From Parker LE, Landstrom AP. Genetic etiology of left-sided obstructive heart lesions: a story in development. J Am Heart Assoc. 2021;10(2):e019006, Fig. 1. https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/epub/10.1161/JAHA.120.019006)."}, {"img": "vfig_p0842_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 37.9", "caption": "Venn diagram showing overlap of genes associated with left-sided obstructive lesions based on disease subtype. Asterisk indicates genes without robust evidence of association. AS, Aortic stenosis; BAV, bicuspid aortic valve; CoA, coarctation of the aorta; HLHS, hyperplastic left heart syndrome; IAA, interrupted aortic arch; LSOL, left-sided obstructive lesion. (From Parker LE, Landstrom AP. Genetic etiology of left-sided obstructive heart lesions: a story in development. J Am Heart Assoc. 2021;10(2):e019006, Fig. 1. https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/epub/10.1161/JAHA.120.019006)."}], "tables_meta": [{"label": "TABLE 37.1", "title": "Genetic Char With Low Pla acteristics, B sma Renin A iochemical Fe ctivity atures, and Treatment for Mono genic Hyperten sion Disorders"}, {"label": "TABLE 37.2", "title": "#### Physical Findings Suggesting a Monogenic Disorder in a Child With Hypertension"}, {"label": "TABLE 37.3", "title": "##### 37.2 #### Physical Findings Suggesting a Monogenic Disorder in a Child With Hypertension—cont’d"}, {"label": "TABLE 37.3", "title": "Genetic Causes of Conge nital Adrenal Hyperplasia and R elation to Hypertension"}, {"label": "Table 37.5", "title": "Syndrome Gene/Genetics Inheritance"}], "body_md": "\n<!-- p.830 -->\n\n# 37 Monogenic Etiologies of Hypertension **YOSUKE MIYASHITA, JOSEPH T. FLYNN** \n\nHypertension has long been identified as a major cause of premature death. In children and adolescents, the global prevalence has increased over the last two decades and is as high as 4%. Hypertension is generally categorized into primary and secondary etiologies. **Primary hypertension** does not have an identifiable etiology, and although genetic factors may play a role, it is largely attributable to body habitus, family history, and lifestyle factors. In contrast, **secondary hypertension** has an identifiable etiology, with kidney disease and renovascular disease being the most common causes in the pediatric age range. Among the causes of secondary hypertension are rare forms attributed to single gene variants inherited in a Mendelian fashion, termed **monogenic hypertension** . Monogenic conditions are categorized into 3 groups: conditions with reduced (or occasionally normal) plasma aldosterone levels, conditions with increased plasma aldosterone levels, and one condition due to vascular smooth muscle proliferation called **hypertension with brachydactyly syndrome** . Table 37.1 summarizes these conditions by causative genetic variants, biochemical features, and targeted therapy. This chapter will only mention hereditary hypertensive conditions such as familial pheochromocytoma, Williams syndrome, neurofibromatosis, or polycystic kidney disease, though a categorization scheme for hypertensive disorders with single-gene determinants, organized by salient biochemical, clinical, and genetic features are presented in Fig. 37.1 and Table 37.2. \n\n## **Monogenic Hypertension With Low Plasma Aldosterone Levels** \n\n### **Liddle Syndrome** \n\nLiddle syndrome is a form of low-renin, low-aldosterone monogenic hypertension caused by pathogenic variants in the epithelial sodium channel (ENaC). These variants create a pathophysiology that mimics primary hyperaldosteronism by leading to aldosterone-independent sodium reabsorption. Patients present with severe hypertension, hypokalemia, metabolic alkalosis, and suppressed plasma renin activity. Inheritance is autosomal dominant. ENaC is \n\nlocated on the apical portion of epithelial cells lining the distal tubules, distal colon, lungs, and exocrine glands. The channel’s expression and activity in the distal tubules are positively regulated by aldosterone and antidiuretic hormone. ENaC is made up of three homologous subunits, α, β, and γ, encoded by _SCNN1A_ , _SCNN1B_ , and _SCNN1G_ , respectively. _SCNN1A_ is located on chromosome 12p13.31, while _SCNN1B_ and _SCNN1G_ are located on chromosome 16p12.2. Sodium ions pass through ENaC from the tubular lumen into the epithelial cell cytoplasm. Together with the renal outer medullary potassium channel (ROMK) and sodium-potassium ATPase, ENaC serves a critical role in maintaining hydroelectrolytic homeostasis related to sodium reabsorption and potassium excretion (Fig. 37.2). \n\nWithin the C-terminus of all three ENaC subunits on the luminal side there is a highly conserved sequence called the proline tyrosine (PY) motif. This PY motif acts as a binding site for E3 ubiquitin ligase family NEDD4 (neural precursor cell <u>expressed, developmentally downregulated 4), which</u> mediates the internalization of ENaC and the proteasomal degradation of the channel. Pathogenic variants in _SCNN1A_ , _SCNN1B_ , or _SCNN1G_ lead to either the loss or the disruption of the PY motif, preventing internalization and degradation of the ENaC and resulting in accumulation of ENaC at the apical membrane of the distal tubule and an increase in the inward sodium current (Fig. 37.2). Additional pathogenic variants increase inward sodium current through mechanisms other than increased ENaC density. For example, one pathogenic variant does not lead to loss or disruption of the PY motif, and therefore, does not affect the density of ENaC, but rather results in an altered extracellular domain of the α unit _(SCNN1A)_ that leads to increased propensity for the channel opening, thus increasing the sodium current into the distal tubular epithelial cells. \n\nLiddle syndrome typically presents during adolescence; it may also present in infancy. Most affected individuals have hypertension; many also have hypokalemia and metabolic alkalosis along with suppressed plasma renin activity and low plasma aldosterone. The family history is typically positive for hypertension and even sudden death; however,\n\n<!-- p.831 -->\n\n\n#### **TABLE 37.1** Genetic Char With Low Pla acteristics, B sma Renin A iochemical Fe ctivity atures, and Treatment for Mono genic Hyperten sion Disorders\n\n||Causative|Inheritance||||||\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n|Condition|Genes|Pattern|PRA|Aldosterone|ARR|Potassium|Treatment|\n|Liddle syndrome|_SCNN1A_<br>_SCNN1B_<br>_SCNN1C_|AD|Low|Low|Low to<br>normal|Low to normal|ENaC antagonist|\n|Gordon syndrome|_WNK1_<br>_WNK2_<br>_CUL3_<br>_KLHL3_|AD<br>AD<br>AD<br>AD and AR|Low|Low to<br>normal|Low to<br>normal|High; rarely will<br>be normal|Thiazide|\n|Syndrome of apparent<br>mineralocorticoid<br>excess|_HSD11B2_|AR|Low|Low|–|Markedly low|MRA|\n|Congenital adrenal<br>hyperplasia|_CYP11B1_<br>_CYP17A1_|AR|Low|Low|–|Variable; low in<br>CYP17A1|MRA|\n|Geller syndrome|_NR3C2_|AD|Low|Low|–|Markedly low|ENaC<br>antagonist,<br>thiazide|\n|Familial<br>hyperaldosteronism<br>type 1<br>(glucocorticoid-<br>remediable<br>aldosteronism)|_CYB11B1/_<br>_CYP11B2_<br>chimeric<br>fusion<br>product|AD|Low|High|High|Mildly low to<br>normal|Low-dose<br>steroids+/−<br>MRA or ENaC<br>antagonist|\n|Familial<br>hyperaldosteronism<br>type 2|_CLCN2_|AD|Low|High|High|Markedly low|MRA|\n|Familial<br>hyperaldosteronism<br>type 3|_KCNJ5_|AD|Low|High|High|Markedly low|MRA and<br>adrenalectomy<br>in severe<br>cases|\n|Familial<br>hyperaldosteronism<br>type 4|_CACNA1H_|AD|Low|High|High|Markedly low|MRA|\n\n\n_AD,_ Autosomal dominant; _AR,_ autosomal recessive; _ARR,_ aldosterone renin ratio; _ENaC,_ epithelial sodium channel; _MRA,_ mineralocorticoid antagonist; _PRA,_ plasma renin activity. \n\nseverity within families with the same pathogenic variant can vary and some family members can be normotensive, suggesting a possible role for environmental factors such as dietary salt intake and additional genetic factors such as polymorphisms that influence sodium handling at the distal tubule. Diagnosis is confirmed by identifying pathogenic variants in _SCNN1A_ , _SCNN1B_ , and _SCNN1G_ . Treatment includes the use of ENaC antagonists such as amiloride or triamterene, as well as implementing a low sodium diet with ideal daily sodium intake below 2 g/day. Blocking ENaC typically improves hypertension, as well as the laboratory abnormalities of hypokalemia, low plasma renin activity, and low aldosterone levels. Some individuals require potassium supplementation and additional antihypertensive agents such as a calcium channel blocker. Loop and thiazide diuretics increase sodium delivery to the distal tubules, \n\nresulting in competition with ENaC antagonists and should be avoided. \n\n### **Gordon Syndrome** \n\nGordon syndrome, also known as **pseudohypoaldosteronism type 2** or **familial hyperkalemia and hypertension syndrome** , is a form of low-renin, low-aldosterone monogenic hypertension that presents with _hyperkalemia_ , in contrast to the low or normal levels of serum potassium seen in patients with most of other forms of monogenic hypertension. Affected individuals also have a persistent metabolic acidosis secondary to a type IV renal tubular acidosis. Individuals with Gordon syndrome have low plasma renin activity and have low aldosterone levels despite high serum potassium levels; aldosterone levels may occasionally be normal.\n\n<!-- p.832 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0833_02.jpg)\n\n\n\n![](figures/vfig_p0832_01.jpg)\n\n**Fig. 37.1** An overview of monogenic hypertension disorders categorized by salient biochemical and genetic features. _APA,_ Aldosterone-producing adenoma; _CAH,_ congenital adrenal hyperplasia; _FH,_ familial hyperaldosteronism; _GRA,_ glucocorticoid remediable aldosteronism; _MEN,_ multiple endocrine neoplasia; _PASNA,_ primary aldosteronism with seizures and neurological abnormalities; _PHA,_ pseudohypoaldosteronism.  (From Fernandez CJ, Pappachan JM, Scholl UI. Monogenic hypertension: an overview. In: _Endocrine Hypertension: From Basic Science to Clinical Practice_ . Philadelphia: Elsevier; 2023 [Fig. 6.1, p.79].) \n\n##### **TABLE 37.2** \n\n#### **Physical Findings Suggesting a Monogenic Disorder in a Child With Hypertension** \n\n|Physical Examination Finding|Genetic Syndrome|Mechanism|Genetic<br>Abnormality Involved|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|Café-au-lait-spots, axillary, or<br>inguinal freckling|Neurofibromatosis type 1|Renal artery stenosis,<br>pheochromocytoma|_NF1_|\n|Hypomelanotic macules, facial<br>angiofibroma, shagreen patch|Tuberous sclerosis complex|Progressive renal failure|_TSC2_ > _TSC1_|\n|Female with short stature, webbed<br>neck, widely spaced nipples,<br>short fourth metacarpal|Turner syndrome|Coarctation of aorta|46, XO|\n|Retinal angioma, spinal or cerebellar<br>hemangioblastoma, adrenal or<br>extra-adrenal pheochromocytoma|Von Hippel-Lindau syndrome|Pheochromocytoma|_VHL_|\n\n\n_(Continued)_\n\n<!-- p.833 -->\n\n##### **TABLE** \n\n##### **37.2** \n\n#### **Physical Findings Suggesting a Monogenic Disorder in a Child With Hypertension—cont’d** \n\n|Physical Examination Finding|Genetic Syndrome|Mechanism|Genetic<br>Abnormality Involved|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|Ambiguous genitalia|Congenital adrenal hyperplasia<br>(11β-hydroxylase deficiency or<br>17α-hydroxylase deficiency)|↑mineralocorticoid<br>agonists|_CYP11B1, CYP17A1_|\n|Short metacarpal bones, short stature|Autosomal dominant hypertension<br>with brachydactyly syndrome|<br>Sodium independent↑<br>vascular resistance|_PDE3A_|\n|Small chin, upturned nose, flattened<br>nose, hypercalcemia, small jaw|William syndrome|Vascular stiffness, artery<br>stenosis|_ELN, NCF1_|\n|Neonatal cholestasis, pulmonic<br>stenosis murmur, posterior<br>embryotoxin, butterfly vertebrae|Alagille syndrome|Renal artery stenosis,<br>mid-aortic coarctation|_JAG1, NOTCH2_|\n\n\nModified from Aggarwal A, Rodriguez-Buritica D. Monogenic hypertension in children: a review with emphasis on genetics. _Adv Chronic Kidney Dis._ 2017;24(6):372-379 (Table 1, p. 37). \n\n\n![](figures/fig_p0834_06.jpg)\n\n\n\n![](figures/vfig_p0833_01.jpg)\n\n**Fig. 37.2** Molecular basis of Liddle syndrome. The epithelial sodium channel (ENaC) regulates sodium reabsorption in the distal tubules of the kidney. (A) ENaC, the renal outer medullary potassium channel (ROMK) and the sodium-potassium ATPase work in concert to maintain hydroelectrolytic balance in renal distal tubule epithelial cells. (B) pathogenic variants in Liddle syndrome result in either increased ENaC density on the apical membrane of the renal distal tubule epithelial cells or an increased propensity for channel opening, resulting in increased inward sodium current and altered flow of potassium, chloride, hydrogen ion, and bicarbonate _(red arrows)_ . Pathophysiology of Liddle syndrome. _AE1,_ Anion exchanger 1; _ATPase,_ pump; _Ca2þ,_ calcium; _Cl_<sup>_2_</sup> , chloride; _ENaC,_ epithelial sodium channel; _Hþ,_ hydrogen ion; _HCO3_<sup>_2_</sup> , bicarbonate; _Kþ,_ potassium; _MR,_ mineralocorticoid receptor; _Naþ,_ sodium; _ROMK,_ renal outer medullary potassium.  (From Fernandez CJ, Pappachan JM, Scholl UI. Monogenic hypertension: an overview. In: _Endocrine Hypertension: From Basic Science to Clinical Practice_ . Philadelphia: Elsevier; 2023 [Fig. 6.3, p. 81].)\n\n<!-- p.834 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0835_01.jpg)\n\n\nGordon syndrome is caused by pathogenic variants in one of 4 different genes, _WNK1_ , _WNK4_ , _CUL3_ , and _KLHL3_ . Phenotypic variation in Gordon syndrome is attributable to the multiple genes in which pathogenic variants can result in disease. Inheritance is autosomal dominant, though there is an additional autosomal recessive form involving _KLHL3_ . The protein product of _WNK4_ combines with the products of _CUL3_ and _KLHL3_ to interact with NCC, ENaC, and the renal outer medullary potassium channel (ROMK) in an inhibitory fashion to maintain normokalemia and normal blood pressure. The protein product of _WNK1_ inhibits the activity of WNK4. on the apical side of tubular epithelial cells. Gordon syndrome can result from pathogenic variants that increase the activity of the kinases produced by either _WNK1_ or _WNK4_ by increasing the activity of two STE20/SPS1-related proline/alanine-rich kinases that are regulated by WNK1 and WNK4. Phosphorylation of these kinases increases NCC activity and the density of the transporter on the apical surface membrane, resulting in increased NCC activity and increased sodium chloride reabsorption. Hyperkalemia in Gordon syndrome arises from a combination of mechanisms. One mechanism is an increase in NCC activity, leading to increased sodium chloride reabsorption in the distal convoluted tubule. The decreased sodium in the collecting ducts reduces the lumen negative potential required for potassium movement through the ROMK in the collecting duct. In addition, WNKs also appear to have a direct effect on ROMK expression, specifically increasing the endocytosis of ROMK through a clathrin-coated pit pathway. Pathogenic _WNK1_ and _WNK4_ variants associated with Gordon syndrome appear to be gain-of-function variants leading to reduction of ROMK channels in the collecting duct, thus decreasing the potassium movement out to the lumen. \n\nPathogenic variants in _CUL3_ and _KLHL3_ disrupt endosomal degradation of WNK, leading to increased NCC activity. Individuals with pathogenic variants in _CUL3_ have the most severe Gordon syndrome phenotype, with very early onset hypertension and severe electrolyte disturbances. The protein product of _CUL3_ is also involved in the degradation of RhoA, a kinase that regulates phosphorylation of vascular smooth muscle myosin light chains. Overaccumulation of RhoA leads to increased vascular smooth muscle vasoconstriction, leading to more severe hypertension. After _CUL3_ , the order of phenotype severity based on gene variant is autosomal recessive _KLHL3_ , autosomal dominant _KLHL3_ , _WNK4_ , and _WNK1_ . Typically, patients with Gordon syndrome caused by _WNK1_ variants can have normal blood pressure into late adulthood. \n\nBiochemically, hyperkalemia can be severe, with serum potassium levels as high as 9 mEq/L. Plasma renin activity is suppressed; aldosterone levels are typically low though can approach normal levels depending on the serum potassium level. Specific treatment of Gordon syndrome includes thiazide diuretics, which block NCC, as well as dietary restriction of sodium and potassium. In mildly affected patients, a low sodium diet alone may be sufficient. \n\n### **Syndrome of Apparent Mineralocorticoid Excess** \n\nThe syndrome of apparent mineralocorticoid excess (AME) is a rare autosomal-recessive form of low-renin, low-aldosterone monogenic hypertension that responds well to mineralocorticoid antagonists. AME is caused by deficiency of 11β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2 (11βHSD2). Both cortisol and aldosterone are ligands for mineralocorticoid receptors. The kidneys receive up to 25% of cardiac output, and plasma free cortisol levels are 100-fold higher than those of aldosterone. 11βHSD2, which is expressed predominantly in mineralocorticoid responsive tissues such as the distal nephron, colon, and salivary glands, converts cortisol to cortisone. In these aldosterone target tissues, 11βHSD2 is expressed at high levels so that the conversion of cortisol to cortisone allows aldosterone to activate mineralocorticoid receptors rather than cortisol. When 11βHSD2 is deficient, cortisol-mineralocorticoid receptor complexes are activated, increasing transepithelial sodium transport. \n\n**Type 1 AME** is more severe due to complete absence of 11βHSD2 activity and typically presents early in life with hypertension, low plasma renin and aldosterone levels, hypokalemia, metabolic alkalosis, and an elevated urinary cortisol:cortisone ratio. The absence of placental 11βHSD2 results in higher in utero cortisol exposure, resulting in low birth weight. Nephrocalcinosis due to hypercalciuria also occurs in most individuals. Due to hypokalemia and hypercalciuria, _secondary nephrogenic diabetes insipidus_ can develop over time, resulting in polyuria and polydipsia. Patients with type 1 AME often have early hypertensive target-organ damage such as left ventricular hypertrophy and retinopathy. \n\n**Type 2 AME** is milder due to varying degrees of residual 11βHSD2 enzyme activity and presents later in life with lower-grade hypertension than in type 1 AME; electrolyte abnormalities are rare or absent. Gene polymorphisms, variations in microsatellite regions, epigenetic modifications, and exogenous and endogenous inhibitors of the 11βHSD2 enzyme are hypothesized to play roles in the milder phenotype. \n\nBiochemical evidence of the syndrome of apparent mineralocorticoid excess consists of an elevated 24-hour urinary free cortisol:cortisone ratio (normal is 0.5). Analysis of cortisol and cortisone metabolites via a 24-hour urinary tetrahydrocortisol + 5α tetrahydrocortisol:tetratrahydrocortisone ratio can also support diagnosis when the ratio is elevated (normal is 1–1.3). The degree to which the ratio of the metabolites is elevated correlates with the residual activity of the 11βHSD2 enzyme. Diagnosis can be confirmed by sequencing of the _HSD11B2_ gene. Treatment consists of mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists such as eplerenone and spironolactone combined with a low sodium and high potassium diet; in severe forms, additional potassium supplementation. Eplerenone is more specific for the mineralocorticoid receptor than spironolactone, sparing the antiandrogenic side effects of spironolactone such as gynecomastia. Eplerenone is not as potent, however, and doses higher than those studied in children may be needed.\n\n<!-- p.835 -->\n\n**Excessive licorice consumption** can induce AME. Licorice contains glycyrrhizic acid and glycyrrhetinic acid, which are potent competitive inhibitors of 11βHSD2 that can cause an acquired form of AME. Overconsumption of grapefruit can also suppress 11βHSD2. Lastly, the high cortisol levels seen in Cushing syndrome can saturate 11βHSD2, resulting in a phenotype resembling AME. \n\n### **Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia** \n\nCongenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH) represents a group of autosomal recessive disorders caused by cortisol biosynthesis defects, several of which are associated with hypertension (Table 37.3). Figure 37.3 presents a schematic diagram of steroidogenesis; phenotypes depend on where \n\n\n#### **TABLE 37.3** Genetic Causes of Conge nital Adrenal Hyperplasia and R elation to Hypertension\n\n||Gene|Chromosome|Affected Organs|Hypertension|\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n|21-hydroxylase deficiency|_CYP21A2_|6p21.1|Adrenal glands|No|\n|11β-hydroxylase deficiency|_CYP11B1_|8q21|Adrenal glands|Yes|\n|17α-hydroxylase/17,20 lyase<br>deficiency|_CYP17A1_|10q24.3|Adrenal glands and gonads|Yes|\n|3β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase<br>type 2 deficiency|_HSD3B2_|1p13.1|Adrenal glands and gonads|No|\n|P450 oxidoreductase deficiency|_POR_|7q11.2|Adrenal glands, gonads, liver,<br>skeletal structures|Yes|\n|Lipoid adrenal hyperplasia|_StAR_|8p11.2|Adrenal glands and gonads|No|\n|Cholesterol side-chain cleavage<br>enzyme deficiency|_CYP11A1_|15q23-q24|Adrenal glands and gonads|No|\n\n\n_POR,_ P450 oxidoreductase; _StAR,_ steroidogenic acute regulatory protein. \n\nModified from Tosun BG, Guran T. Congenital adrenal hyperplasia and hypertension. In: _Endocrine Hypertension: From Basic Science to Clinical Practice_ , Philadelphia: Elsevier; 2023 (Table 9.1, p. 116). \n\n\n![](figures/fig_p0836_07.jpg)\n\n\n\n![](figures/vfig_p0835_01.jpg)\n\n**Fig. 37.3** Adrenal steroidogenesis. _DOC,_ Deoxycorticosterone; _DHEA,_ dehydroepiandrosterone, _StAR,_ steroidogenic acute regulatory protein. Enzymes whose deficiency is associated with hypertension are illustrated in _red_ . *17-hydroxylase, 17,20 lyase, and 21-hydroxylase depend on P450 oxidoreductase (POR) for their catalytic activities.  (From Tosun BG, Guran T. Congenital adrenal hyperplasia and hypertension. In: _Endocrine Hypertension: From Basic Science to Clinical Practice_ . Philadelphia: Elsevier; 2023 [Fig. 9.1, p.116].)\n\n<!-- p.836 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0837_02.jpg)\n\n\n\n![](figures/vfig_p0836_01.jpg)\n\n**Fig. 37.4** Adrenal steroidogenesis in 11β-hydroxylase deficiency. Font size shows relative increases and decreases in production.  (From Tosun BG, Guran T. Congenital adrenal hyperplasia and hypertension. In: _Endocrine Hypertension: From Basic Science to Clinical Practice_ . Philadelphia: Elsevier; 2023 [Fig. 9.2, p.116].) \n\nin the pathway an enzyme defect occurs, what shunting effects result, and which steroids are overproduced due to negative feedback mechanisms. The most common defect, 21-hydroxylase deficiency, which accounts for up to 90% of all CAH cases, _does not_ cause hypertension. The defects that can result in hypertension are **11 b-hydroxylase deficiency (11 b-OHD) and cytochrome P450 17A1 enzyme deficiency (combined 17-hydroxylase/17, 20- lyase deficiency and isolated 17, 20 lyase deficiency)** . 11 β-OHD accounts for up to 8% of all CAH. \n\n_CYP11B2_ encodes the 11 β-hydroxylase protein, which is responsible for conversion of 11-deoxycortisol to cortisol in the zona fasciculata as the final step in cortisol synthesis, and the conversion of 11-deoxycorticosterone to corticosterone one step prior to aldosterone formation in the zona glomerulosa (Fig. 37.3). 11 β-OHD results in decreased formation of cortisol and corticosterone (Fig. 37.4). The low cortisol level leads to an increase in corticotropin production via a negative feedback mechanism in the pituitary-adrenal axis, causing overproduction of steroid precursors preceding the 11 β-hydroxylase step. In the face of 11 β-OHD, the overproduced steroid precursors are shunted toward androgen production, leading to _virilization_ in 46, XX patients and _precocious puberty_ in 46, XY patients. Hypertension occurs in up to two-thirds of patients with 11 β-OHD. The mechanism of hypertension is unclear though is believed to be due to mineralocorticoid precursor excess despite there being no firm correlation between blood pressure and serum deoxycorticosterone levels. Hypokalemia is the result of excess mineralocorticoid activity and has not been shown to \n\ncorrelate with hypertension. Phenotypes vary based on the level of residual enzyme deficiency, but classically, 46, XX newborns present with clitoral enlargement and labioscrotal fusion. If not diagnosed as newborns, children can present with premature axillary and pubic hair development, premature somatic growth, and advanced bone age. \n\n_CYP17A1_ , located on chromosome 10, produces cytochrome P450 17A1, which catalyzes both the 17 α-hydroxylase reaction and the 17,20-lyase reaction (Fig. 37.5); most defects in this enzyme cause combined deficiencies in both reactions. 17 α-hydroxylase generates 17-hydroxypregnenolone and 17-hydroxyprogesterone, which ultimately can be converted to cortisol or to DHEA and androstenedione, which serve as precursors for androgen and estrogen synthesis. Defects in 17 α-hydroxylase effectively shunt steroidogenesis toward mineralocorticoid production, and because 17 α-hydroxylase acts early in the pathway, its deficiency leads to very little production of sex hormones. 46, XY individuals typically present with ambiguous external genitalia or a female phenotype, while 46, XX individuals present with primary amenorrhea and absent or delayed secondary sexual characteristics. Typically, in adolescence, severe hypertension with hypokalemia occurs due to deoxycorticosterone excess. Diagnosis is suggested by abnormal ACTH stimulation testing and by plasma steroid analysis revealing abnormally high levels of pregnenolone and progesterone, especially relative to 17-hydroxypregnenolone and 17-hydroxyprogesterone levels, respectively. Hypertension and hypokalemia respond well to mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists such as\n\n<!-- p.837 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0838_01.jpg)\n\n\n\n![](figures/vfig_p0837_01.jpg)\n\n**Fig. 37.5** Adrenal steroidogenesis in 17-α-hydroxylase deficiency. Font size shows relative increases and decreases in production.  (From Tosun BG, Guran T. Congenital adrenal hyperplasia and hypertension. In _Endocrine Hypertension: From Basic Science to Clinical Practice._ Philadelphia: Elsevier; 2023 [Fig. 9.3, p.118].) \n\nspironolactone and eplerenone. Despite cytochrome P450 17A1 enzyme deficiency leading steroidogenesis to shunt towards the mineralocorticoid pathway, resulting in high amounts of its precursors, the final product of the pathway, aldosterone, is low. Appropriate compensatory activation of the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis driving increased production of deoxycorticosterone, a weak mineralocorticoid, is believed to underlie the low aldosterone levels. \n\n### **Geller Syndrome** \n\nGeller syndrome has been alternately labeled as _hypertension exacerbated by pregnancy_ , which is a misnomer, as this condition is not limited to pregnant females. Rather, this rare autosomal dominant condition is caused by a missense variant in _NR3C2_ that alters the hormone-binding domain of mineralocorticoid receptors, resulting in constitutive activation of the receptor not only by aldosterone, but also by cortisone and progesterone. The receptor is also activated by mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists such as spironolactone. Symptoms can develop at an early age with signs of mineralocorticoid excess, including hypertension, hypokalemia, and low plasma renin and aldosterone levels. Hypertension is more severe during pregnancy because the altered mineralocorticoid receptor is activated by non-mineralocorticoid steroids such as progesterone, levels of which are about 100-fold higher during pregnancy. In nonpregnant affected individuals, cortisone produced by _11bHSD2_ binds to and activates mineralocorticoid receptors, presumably leading to hypertension. Treatment includes thiazide diuretics, ENaC \n\ninhibitors, and a low sodium diet. Mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists should be avoided. \n\n## **Monogenic Hypertension With Elevated Plasma Aldosterone** \n\n### **Glucocorticoid-Remediable Aldosteronism** \n\nGlucocorticoid-remediable aldosteronism (GRA) is also known as **familial hyperaldosteronism type 1** , aldosterone synthase hyperactivity, and glucocorticoid suppressible hyperaldosteronism. GRA is an autosomal dominant form of primary hyperaldosteronism that causes hypertension with high plasma aldosterone and low renin levels, along with abnormal adrenal steroid production. \n\nGlucocorticoids, mineralocorticoids, and androgens are steroid hormones that share the same biosynthetic pathways (Fig. 37.3). One of the primary drivers of aldosterone production is high plasma potassium concentration. Additional stimuli include hypotension (sensed by stretch receptors in the heart), acidosis, and increased circulating angiotensin II. Adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH) typically has only a minor role in regulating aldosterone production. \n\nThe genes that encode 11β-hydroxylase _(CYP11B1)_ and aldosterone synthase _(CYP11B2)_ are both located on chromosome 8 within proximity of each other. The two genes share 95% sequence homology and have the same intronexon structures. GRA results from an unequal crossover during DNA replication. The 5′ regulatory region of _CYP11B1_ inserts in the coding region of _CYP11B2_ . The new chimeric gene now encodes for an aldosterone synthase\n\n<!-- p.838 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0839_02.jpg)\n\n\n\n![](figures/vfig_p0838_01.jpg)\n\n**Fig. 37.6** Chimeric gene formation in glucocorticoid-remediable aldosteronism results from an unequal crossover event. The genes that encode aldosterone synthase _(CYP11B2)_ and 11 β-hydroxylase: _(CYP11B1)_ are both located on chromosome 8 within proximity of each other. The 5′ ACTH-responsive promoter region of the 11β-hydroxylase gene is fused to the 3′ coding sequence of the aldosterone synthase gene. As a result, aldosterone synthesis is abnormally regulated by ACTH rather than by the renin-angiotensin system.  (From Halperin F, Dluhy RG. Glucocorticoid-remediable aldosteronism. _Endocrinol Metab Clin North Am._ 2011;40(2):333-341 [Fig. 2, p. 337].) \n\nthat responds not only to its typical stimuli, but also to ACTH in the zona fasciculata (Fig. 37.6). In GRA, ACTH stimulates production of both cortisol and aldosterone resulting in a significant increase in the aldosterone concentration. The increased aldosterone levels upregulate ENaC and sodium potassium-ATPase expression in the collecting ducts of the kidneys, leading to increased sodium and water reabsorption, increased blood pressure, and increased urinary potassium excretion. \n\nSymptoms typically develop early in life. Several aspects of GRA are poorly understood. First, serum potassium levels can be normal or only mildly decreased despite elevated aldosterone levels. Second, affected individuals have a high risk of hemorrhagic stroke at an early age. The diagnosis is suggested by a high ratio of aldosterone to plasma renin, as well as demonstration of elevated urine 18-oxocortisol and 18-hydroxycortisol levels that suppress after a dexamethasone suppression test. Diagnosis is confirmed via genetic testing that identifies the chimeric gene product. Treatment consists of low-dose dexamethasone administered in the evenings to suppress ACTH secretion, thereby reducing aldosterone production. Typically, doses of 0.125–1.0 mg/ day are sufficient, but individuals may experience Cushingoid effects even at these doses. Mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists such as spironolactone or eplerenone may need to be added to dexamethasone to block the effects of aldosterone. For individuals unwilling or unable to tolerate dexamethasone, a combination of calcium channel blockers and mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists may be used. \n\n### **Familial Hyperaldosteronism Type 2** \n\nFamilial hyperaldosteronism type 2 is an autosomal dominant form of primary hyperaldosteronism. _The lack of a response to dexamethasone challenge distinguishes this condition from GRA_ . _CLCN2_ , located on chromosome 7, encodes the voltage gated chloride channel ClC-2 in the adrenal zona \n\nglomerulosa. Gain-of-function variants in _CLCN2_ lower the threshold of membrane depolarization, resulting in increased calcium influx, upregulation of _CYP11B2_ gene expression, and increased aldosterone production. Familial hyperaldosteronism type 2 is characterized by bilateral adrenocortical disease and adenomas and is clinically indistinguishable from non-inherited primary hyperaldosteronism. Thus, obtaining family history and genetic testing for _CLCN2_ variants is critical for proper diagnosis. Treatment consists of mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists or unilateral adrenalectomy. \n\n### **Familial Hyperaldosteronism Type 3** \n\nFamilial hyperaldosteronism type 3 is a rare autosomal dominant condition accounting for up to 0.4% of primary hyperaldosteronism. _KCNJ5_ encodes the inwardly rectifying potassium channel Kir3.4, which is expressed in adrenal cortical cells. Pathogenic variants in _KCNJ5_ lead to loss of selectivity of the Kir3.4 pore, resulting in sodium influx and depolarization of the cell membrane, which increases _CYP11B2_ expression and aldosterone production. Affected individuals present with bilateral adrenal hyperplasia, severe hypertension, and hypokalemia. Treatment consists of adrenalectomy and mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists. \n\n### **Familial Hyperaldosteronism Type 4** \n\nFamilial hyperaldosteronism type 4 is an autosomal dominant form of primary hyperaldosteronism caused by pathogenic gain of function variants in _CACNA1H_ , a gene abundantly expressed in the adrenal zona glomerulosa that encodes the ɑ-subunit of a T-type calcium channel called Cav3.2. Pathogenic variants in this gene lead to increased aldosterone levels, resulting in a phenotype similar to other familial hyperaldosteronism syndromes, with hypertension and hypokalemia. There is no specific treatment for this disorder; adrenalectomy or mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists are used.\n\n<!-- p.839 -->\n\n## **Autosomal Dominant Hypertension With Brachydactyly** \n\nAutosomal dominant hypertension with brachydactyly is a rare disorder characterized by brachydactyly type E (i.e., thickening and shortening of metacarpals and/or phalanges), short stature, and severe salt-independent hypertension. Candidate genes include _PDE3A_ and _SUR2_ ; phosphodiesterases such as the one encoded by _PDE3A_ play vital roles in many cellular processes and are prominently expressed in platelets, oocytes, cardiac myocytes, and vascular smooth muscle cells, and are also involved in the early stages of osteogenesis. Gain-of-function missense variants in _PDE3A_ have been associated with neointimal proliferation and remodeling of \n\narteries and neurovascular structures. Affected individuals develop progressive hypertension starting in childhood. Hypertension is believed to be secondary to vasoconstriction and increased peripheral vascular resistance, while skeletal changes are believed to be secondary to dysregulation of parathyroid-related protein. Vascular abnormalities include unilateral or bilateral posterior inferior cerebellar artery or vertebral artery loops that contact the ventrolateral medulla in the posterior fossa, which is believed to contribute to barodysregulation. If untreated, stroke and early mortality result (Fig. 37.7). Early treatment can reduce the chance of stroke. Single agent or combination therapy with β-blockers, α-blockers, calcium channel blockers, and angiotensin converting enzyme inhibitors has been recommended. \n\n\n![](figures/fig_p0840_04.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 37.7** Clinical and imaging findings in autosomal dominant hypertension with brachydactyly syndrome. (A) Severe brachydactyly is observed. (B) Radiograph of the hands shows a short length of the metacarpal bones in both hands and cone-shaped epiphyses. (C) Brain magnetic resonance angiogram (MRA) of the patient performed for headaches at 35 years of age, with right posterior cerebral artery (PCA) stenosis and left intracranial vertebral artery hypoplasia. (D–F) Brain diffusion-weighted imaging (DWI) performed at the first stroke event (at 42 years of age) shows a right pontine infarct, and MRA reveals stenosis of the basilar artery (BA) and bilateral PCA. (G and H) Repeat DWI obtained 1 day after stroke onset show additional infarct in the right thalamus and a gradient-recalled echo image reveals blooming artifact in the BA. _(Continued)_\n\n<!-- p.840 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0841_02.jpg)\n\n\n**• Fig. 37.7, cont’d** (I and J) Repeat DWI performed two weeks after admission shows newly developed acute infarctions in the left thalamus, left basal ganglia, and left temporal lobe. (K–N) Repeat DWI obtained the next day shows progression of the initial infarct in the left temporal lobe extending to left parietal lobe, and T2-and T1-weighted images show intramural hematoma in the BA and bilateral PCA. (O) Repeat brain MRA reveals progression of stenosis in the BA and bilateral PCA and occurrence of left middle cerebral artery stenosis. These abnormal arteries had regions of both dilation and constriction.  (From Lee CG, Kang K, Yoon RG, et al. PDE3A variant associated with hypertension and brachydactyly syndrome in a patient with ischemic stroke caused by spontaneous intracranial artery dissection: a review of the clinical and molecular genetic features. _Eur J Med Genetics_ . 2020;63(4) [Fig. 2].) \n\n## **When to Consider Evaluation for Monogenic Hypertension** \n\nWith rising rates of child and adolescent primary hypertension, it is difficult to know when to evaluate for monogenic hypertension, though the onset of hypertension in childhood especially preadolescence, should warrant such consideration. Family history may reveal helpful indicators such as multiple family members with early-onset hypertension, premature cardiovascular events, or both. Traditionally, and more typically for adult hypertension, the severity of the hypertension, the degree of serum electrolyte abnormalities (particularly abnormal potassium levels), and the response to treatment are considered indicators of heritable secondary forms of hypertension. However, many children with monogenic hypertension have only mild to moderate elevation in blood pressures. \n\nThe initial approach should include a review of history for disorders of sexual development, delayed or precocious puberty, polyuria, muscle weakness, family history of difficult to treat early-onset hypertension, and early \n\ncardiovascular events and/or sudden death. Physical examination should assess for findings of ambiguous genitalia, virilization, and short stature, and exclude obesity and other lifestyle-related features (Table 37.2). Laboratory review should include screening for any subtle electrolyte and acid-base disturbances. _More common forms of secondary hypertension such as renal parenchymal disease, renovascular disease, coarctation of aorta, and neuroendocrine tumors should be excluded._ Finally, lack of expected efficacy to first line antihypertensive medical treatment can also be used to determine the index of suspicion for monogenic hypertension. \n\nExcept for autosomal dominant hypertension with brachydactyly, these monogenic hypertension etiologies share two features that result in expanded extracellular volume. First is that single gene variants all lead to increased sodium reabsorption in the distal nephron. Second, _plasma renin activity in all cases is suppressed_ , often below the limits of detection. In the setting of hypertension and suppressed plasma renin activity, further evaluation including targeted genetic testing should be pursued (Fig. 37.8).\n\n<!-- p.841 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0842_01.jpg)\n\n\n\n![](figures/vfig_p0841_01.jpg)\n\n**Fig. 37.8** A diagnostic algorithm for early-onset hypertension. Determining plasma renin levels dictates consideration of monogenic hypertension: elevated renin levels suggest renovascular and other nonmonogenic forms of hypertension. Normal renin levels suggest familial pheochromocytoma/ paraganglioma or autosomal dominant hypertension with brachydactyly syndrome. Low renin levels are seen in the other forms of monogenic hypertension. When not specifically stated, any medications listed in the algorithm indicate a trial to assess responsiveness to the medication. _AME,_ Apparent mineralocorticoid excess; _CAH,_ congenital adrenal hyperplasia; _DOC,_ deoxycorticosterone; _FH,_ familial hyperaldosteronism; _FHHt,_ familial hyperkalemia and hypertension syndrome (Gordon syndrome); _GRA,_ glucocorticoid-remediable aldosteronism; _HTNB,_ autosomal dominant hypertension with brachydactyly syndrome; _MRA,_ mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist; _PASNA,_ primary aldosteronism, seizures, and neurological abnormalities; _PPGL,_ pheochromocytoma/paraganglioma.  (From Fernandez CJ, Pappachan JM, Scholl UI. Monogenic hypertension: an overview. In: _Endocrine Hypertension: From Basic Science to Clinical Practice_ . Philadelphia: Elsevier; 2023 [Fig. 6.8, p. 84].) \n\n## **Genes Associated With Syndromic and Nonsyndromic Coarctation of the Aorta** \n\nAlthough not traditionally included in a discussion of monogenic etiologies of hypertension, specific genes have been reported in patients with coarctation of the aorta (Table 37.4). Many of these genes overlap with other leftsided obstructive cardiac lesions (Fig. 37.9). \n\nCoarctation may present with upper extremity hypertension and a gradient producing postcoarctation lower extremity decreased blood pressures and claudication. Complex coarctation in the newborn that is associated with other cardiac defects may produce heart failure with respiratory distress. Femoral pulses will be weak or absent and the preductal to postductal blood pressure gradient is usually >35 mm Hg. \n\n#### **TABLE Syndromes and Genes Associated With 37.4 Coarctation of the Aorta** \n\n|Syndrome|Gene/Genetics|Inheritance|\n|---|---|---|\n|Noonan 1|_PTPN11_|AD|\n|Kabuki|_KMT2D_|AD|\n|Myhre|_SMAD4_|AD|\n|Floating-Harbor|_SRCAP_|AD|\n|Orstavik-<br>Lindemann-<br>Solberg*|_WDPCP_|AR|\n|Turner|XO,_TIMP1, TIMP3_|Haploinsufficiency|\n|Nonsyndromic|_MYH6_|Variable<br>penetrance|\n\n\n*Also called congenital heart defects, tongue hamartomas, polysyndactyly syndrome.\n\n<!-- p.842 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0843_02.jpg)\n\n\n\n![](figures/vfig_p0842_01.jpg)\n\n**Fig. 37.9** Venn diagram showing overlap of genes associated with left-sided obstructive lesions based on disease subtype. Asterisk indicates genes without robust evidence of association. _AS,_ Aortic stenosis; _BAV,_ bicuspid aortic valve; _CoA,_ coarctation of the aorta; _HLHS,_ hyperplastic left heart syndrome; _IAA,_ interrupted aortic arch; _LSOL,_ left-sided obstructive lesion.  (From Parker LE, Landstrom AP. Genetic etiology of left-sided obstructive heart lesions: a story in development. _J Am Heart Assoc._ 2021;10(2):e019006, Fig. 1. https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/epub/10.1161/JAHA.120.019006). \n"});
